<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ebussuud Efendi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1490">1490</a>–<a href="/years/1574">1574</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mehmed Ebüssuûd Efendi, El-İmâdî</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ebussuud Efendi was an Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> jurist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> exegete who served as qadi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> from 1533 to 1537 and as Shaykh al-Islām, the empire's supreme judge, from 1545 until his death. Working closely with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a>, he built a legal framework joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> with the sultan's administrative law (qānūn), under which judges derived their power from the sultan and had to follow his law-letters. He systematised the issuing of fatwās and wrote thousands of them: some legitimised Suleiman's killings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> and declared the killing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> praiseworthy, others allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and Karagöz plays. He completed a commentary on the whole Quran in 1566.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ebussuud-efendi.jpg" alt="Ebussuud Efendi" width="330" height="302" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christies.com · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Abu_al-Su'ud_(Abu_Su'ud_Efendi)_(d._1574);_Du'anama_(Book_of_Prayers)_signed_Muhammad_Amin_al-Husayni_al-Tirmidhi,_Ottoman_Turkey,_dated_1599-1600.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ebussuud Efendi was born on 30 December 1490, the son of Iskilipli Sheikh Muhiddin Muhammad Efendi. He was also called El-İmâdî because his family came from Imâd, a village near İskilip. In the 1530s he served as a judge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and Rumelia, where he brought local laws into line with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>, before Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> made him Shaykh al-Islām in 1545. He held that office until his death on 23 August 1574 and brought it to the peak of its power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As Shaykh al-Islām Ebussuud turned the issuing of fatwās, until then largely informal, into an organised procedure. He set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, the fatwā-khāna, in which scribes (fatwā katibi) with legal training drafted questions to fixed technical and rhetorical standards and clerks (fatwā emini), scholars holding senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> posts, sometimes prepared provisional answers before they reached him. His manual Advice to Fetva Scribes (Fetva Katiblerine Tenbih) taught how to frame a query so that it could be answered precisely and in the fewest words. The reform gave Ottoman fatwā documents a standard style and let the office issue them in large numbers.</p><p>His rulings covered ritual prayer, ablution, contracts, religious foundations, the practices of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a>, family matters and the affairs of non-Muslim communities. He shaped the imperial land and tax systems in line with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> tradition and the institution of the cash-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waqf">waqf</a>. His opinions did not always side with the state: he declared that collecting poll tax from landless peasants, torturing suspects and taxing pigs went against religious law. He also issued rulings that legitimised the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> by Suleiman's successor Selim, and a contested fatwā permitting dealings involving interest (riba) in certain cases, which modern Muslim modernists often cite.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His fatwā collections, known as Ma‘ruḍāt and also as Fetava-yı Ebussuud, survive in several versions compiled by different hands in Turkish archives and libraries, and were cited by generations of later jurists. His Qur'an commentary, Irshādu-l-‘Aqqli-s-Saleem ilā Mazāyā Al-Kitaabi-l-Kareem, earned him awards from Suleiman, who sent copies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>; later scholars such as Muhammad Taqi Usmani count it among the four leading commentaries. He also wrote on mysticism, Arabic grammar, literature and poetry, and commentaries on al-Taftazānī's Talweeḥ and al-Nasafī's Manār.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ebussuud Efendi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ebussuud Efendi</b>, şeyhülislam from 1545 to 1574, worked with Suleiman the Magnificent to reconcile the sultan’s law (kanun) with the sharia, and issued thousands of legal opinions (fatwas), on matters from land tenure to the drinking of coffee.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in İskilip, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.73,34.47&amp;year=1544" aria-label="İskilip on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5395 709 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ebussuud Efendi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5845 979h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5845 979h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.73,34.47&amp;year=1544">What was here in 1544? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
